WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.775
Second block.

2
00:00:01.775 --> 00:00:10.650
The algorithm finds the best alignment for a scoring scheme — so now let us look hard at the scoring scheme, which is where the biology actually lives.

3
00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:16.850
Until now the gap penalty has been one flat number, g, charged per gap position.

4
00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:19.700
That choice matters far more than it looks.

5
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:24.800
A linear penalty means n consecutive gaps cost n times g-open.

6
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:35.000
Do the arithmetic and notice what it implies: the total is identical whether those gap positions are bunched into one block or scattered one at a time across the alignment.

7
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.350
Five single gaps cost exactly what one gap of five costs.

8
00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:42.250
The scheme has no opinion about it.

9
00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:44.825
Biology does have an opinion.

10
00:00:44.825 --> 00:00:52.025
Insertions and deletions are mutational events, and one event usually affects a contiguous stretch of residues.

11
00:00:52.025 --> 00:00:56.550
A single insertion of five residues is one thing that happened.

12
00:00:56.550 --> 00:01:03.750
Five separate single-residue insertions are five things that happened, and five events are far less likely than one.

13
00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:09.400
A scoring scheme indifferent between those will cheerfully produce the implausible version.

14
00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:17.150
It sprinkles single gaps wherever they buy a point or two, and in doing so it breaks up blocks that evolution conserved as units.

15
00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:20.575
The top pair on this slide shows exactly that.

16
00:01:20.575 --> 00:01:27.050
Scattered gaps, and the shared Q-Q-W motif has been chopped apart to squeeze out a slightly better score.

17
00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:30.525
Every individual choice was locally rational.

18
00:01:30.525 --> 00:01:33.725
The result is not a believable history.

19
00:01:34.325 --> 00:01:40.950
The fix is an affine gap penalty, and it works by charging for the event rather than for the positions.

20
00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:44.500
Opening a new gap costs a large amount, g-open.

21
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:49.075
Extending a gap that is already open costs much less, g-extend.

22
00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:54.300
So a gap of length n costs g-open plus n minus one times g-extend.

23
00:01:54.300 --> 00:02:02.650
The first position is expensive because it represents an event; each further position is cheap because it is the same event continuing.

24
00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:11.250
With gap open minus ten and extend minus zero point five, a ten-residue gap as one block costs minus fourteen point five.

25
00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:19.375
Compare that with charging minus ten at every position, which is the linear scheme with the same opening cost: minus one hundred.

26
00:02:19.375 --> 00:02:28.025
The affine scheme has made the single long gap roughly seven times cheaper than the scattered equivalent, which is precisely the preference we wanted.

27
00:02:28.025 --> 00:02:33.400
The bottom pair on the slide is the same two sequences under affine penalties.

28
00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:39.975
The gaps have consolidated into contiguous stretches and the Q-Q-W motif has stayed intact.

29
00:02:39.975 --> 00:02:47.100
Same sequences, same algorithm, different gap model — and now the alignment proposes a history you can believe.

30
00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:50.875
Affine penalties are what every real tool uses.

31
00:02:50.875 --> 00:02:53.625
The defaults are usually sensible.

32
00:02:53.625 --> 00:02:59.225
But they are parameters, and you will see in the lab how much the answer moves when you change them.

33
00:02:59.825 --> 00:03:06.600
The other half of the score is the substitution score — what we get for aligning residue a against residue b.

34
00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:11.700
Treating all mismatches alike throws away most of what we know about proteins.

35
00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:17.875
Real substitution scores are log-odds scores, the same construction as Session one's log-odds.

36
00:03:17.875 --> 00:03:29.475
Roughly: take how often a and b are seen aligned in genuinely related proteins, divide by how often they would be paired by chance given how common each residue is, and take the logarithm.

37
00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:32.075
The ratio is the whole point.

38
00:03:32.075 --> 00:03:40.200
A positive score means the pair turns up among relatives more often than chance predicts — so seeing it is evidence for relatedness.

39
00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:45.225
A negative score means it turns up less often than chance — evidence against.

40
00:03:45.225 --> 00:03:55.200
Conservative swaps like lysine for arginine, or isoleucine for valine, come out positive, because they happen in real families without breaking the protein.

41
00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:59.275
And the denominator explains something that surprises people.

42
00:03:59.275 --> 00:04:06.775
Tryptophan against tryptophan scores plus seventeen in BLOSUM sixty-two, far higher than most identities.

43
00:04:06.775 --> 00:04:10.125
That is not because tryptophan is important.

44
00:04:10.125 --> 00:04:19.075
It is because tryptophan is rare, so two sequences both having one in the same column is unlikely by chance and therefore highly informative.

45
00:04:19.075 --> 00:04:21.700
Rarity carries information.

46
00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:27.800
Session five builds profiles on exactly this idea, applied one alignment column at a time.

47
00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.650
Here is BLOSUM sixty-two in full.

48
00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:37.050
Rather than reading off numbers, look at the structure, because the structure is chemistry.

49
00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:43.000
The diagonal is identities, and it is the highest-scoring part of the matrix — unsurprising.

50
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:47.075
What is more interesting is that the diagonal is not flat.

51
00:04:47.075 --> 00:04:52.525
Rare residues score higher on it than common ones, for the reason we just discussed.

52
00:04:52.525 --> 00:04:56.375
Off the diagonal, positive scores cluster into blocks.

53
00:04:56.375 --> 00:05:04.750
There is a hydrophobic block — alanine, valine, isoleucine, leucine, methionine — where the members substitute for one another readily.

54
00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:09.475
There is an aromatic block of phenylalanine, tryptophan and tyrosine.

55
00:05:09.475 --> 00:05:14.200
Lysine with arginine, the two long basic residues, is positive.

56
00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:18.475
Aspartate with glutamate, the two acidic ones, is positive.

57
00:05:18.475 --> 00:05:20.950
Nobody put the chemistry in.

58
00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:26.800
These matrices were built by counting substitutions in real alignments of real proteins.

59
00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:35.050
The blocks emerged because evolution tolerates swaps that preserve a residue's chemical role and does not tolerate swaps that destroy it.

60
00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:39.800
The matrix is a record of what proteins have actually been able to survive.

61
00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:47.375
Two families of substitution matrix dominate, and they differ in the data they come from and in the model behind them.

62
00:05:47.375 --> 00:05:59.500
PAM — Point Accepted Mutations, from Dayhoff, Schwartz and Orcutt in nineteen seventy-eight — is built from global alignments of closely related proteins, with an explicit evolutionary model.

63
00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:08.725
The sequences are arranged into phylogenetic trees, ancestral sequences are inferred, and substitutions are counted along the tree's branches.

64
00:06:08.725 --> 00:06:11.250
Three quantities do the work.

65
00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:15.100
A residue's frequency is how often it occurs at all.

66
00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:26.800
Its mutability is how often it was seen to change, divided by how often it could have changed — and the first surprise in Dayhoff's paper is that frequency and mutability do not track each other.

67
00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:43.200
Dividing an observed rate by what chance alone would give is an odds ratio; its logarithm turns "rarer than chance" into a negative number and "commoner than chance" into a positive one, which is why every substitution matrix holds log-odds rather than probabilities.

68
00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:54.850
Matrices for greater distances are then extrapolated by matrix multiplication: PAM two is PAM one applied twice, PAM two-fifty is PAM one applied two hundred and fifty times.

69
00:06:54.850 --> 00:07:03.825
BLOSUM — BLOcks SUbstitution Matrix, Henikoff and Henikoff, nineteen ninety-two — takes the opposite approach on every count.

70
00:07:03.825 --> 00:07:07.625
Local, ungapped alignments instead of global ones.

71
00:07:07.625 --> 00:07:11.475
Distantly related proteins instead of close ones.

72
00:07:11.475 --> 00:07:21.800
Substitution frequencies counted directly from conserved blocks of real multiple alignments, with no tree, no inferred ancestors, and no extrapolation.

73
00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:27.150
Each BLOSUM matrix is derived independently from its own data.

74
00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:30.450
That independence is the practical difference.

75
00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:40.825
A PAM matrix for distant relatives is an extrapolation from close ones, and it inherits whatever the model got wrong, amplified two hundred and fifty times over.

76
00:07:40.825 --> 00:07:46.525
A BLOSUM matrix for distant relatives was counted from distant relatives.

77
00:07:47.125 --> 00:07:55.975
One thing to memorise, because it is the most reliable way to get a search quietly wrong: the two numbering schemes run in opposite directions.

78
00:07:55.975 --> 00:07:59.650
A higher PAM number means a more distant comparison.

79
00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:05.175
PAM two-fifty is for divergent sequences, PAM one hundred for closer ones.

80
00:08:05.175 --> 00:08:13.800
It follows directly from the construction — the number counts how many times the one-step matrix was applied, so more evolutionary time.

81
00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:17.775
A higher BLOSUM number means a closer comparison.

82
00:08:17.775 --> 00:08:24.525
BLOSUM eighty is for similar sequences, BLOSUM forty-five for divergent ones.

83
00:08:24.525 --> 00:08:31.000
That number is the identity threshold at which sequences were clustered when the matrix was built.

84
00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:39.525
So PAM two-fifty and BLOSUM forty-five do a similar job, and PAM one hundred and BLOSUM eighty do a similar job.

85
00:08:39.525 --> 00:08:42.700
High PAM goes with low BLOSUM.

86
00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:51.375
The scales are not aligned with each other and there is no reason they should be, since they were built by different people from different data for different purposes.

87
00:08:51.375 --> 00:08:58.575
Why it matters: picking a matrix built for close relatives when you are hunting distant ones does not produce an error.

88
00:08:58.575 --> 00:09:05.975
It produces a search that finds nothing, and silence looks the same whether there is nothing there or you asked the wrong question.

89
00:09:05.975 --> 00:09:18.525
BLOSUM sixty-two is BLAST's default in Session five, and it is a reasonable middle-of-the-road choice — which is why it is the default, and why it is also the wrong choice for the hardest cases.

90
00:09:19.125 --> 00:09:27.300
Here is global against local on two real fragments — one from beta-globin, one from myoglobin — scored with PAM two-fifty.

91
00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:32.275
These two proteins are genuinely related but have diverged a long way.

92
00:09:32.275 --> 00:09:39.725
The global alignment has to use all of S-A-V-T-A-L-W-G-K-V-N and all of Q-L-V-L-N-V-W-G-K-V-E.

93
00:09:39.725 --> 00:09:42.325
Every residue must appear.

94
00:09:42.325 --> 00:09:44.575
It scores thirty-three.

95
00:09:44.575 --> 00:09:52.175
The local alignment is free to drop the beginnings, which do not correspond well, and keep the shared W-G-K-V region.

96
00:09:52.175 --> 00:09:54.350
It scores forty-three.

97
00:09:54.350 --> 00:09:58.725
Now, the question worth sitting with: why is the local score higher?

98
00:09:58.725 --> 00:10:04.775
Both algorithms are finding an optimum, and the local alignment is a subset of the global one.

99
00:10:04.775 --> 00:10:07.300
How can taking less give more?

100
00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:10.600
Because the first few aligned pairs cost points.

101
00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:19.975
S against Q, A against L — these are poorly scoring pairs under PAM two-fifty, and the global alignment has no choice but to include them and pay.

102
00:10:19.975 --> 00:10:28.025
The local alignment simply declines, starts where the correspondence actually begins, and keeps only the part carrying signal.

103
00:10:28.025 --> 00:10:30.800
Look also at the two matrices.

104
00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:35.950
The local one has no negative numbers in it at all — that is the flooring at zero.

105
00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:41.900
Its path starts at the highest cell and stops at a zero, rather than running corner to corner.

106
00:10:41.900 --> 00:10:50.975
The same two sequences, the same matrix, the same gap penalties: two different questions, two different answers, and the local one found the biology.

107
00:10:51.575 --> 00:10:54.875
We now have a score for any pair of sequences.

108
00:10:54.875 --> 00:11:00.425
The uncomfortable question is what a score means, and the answer is: on its own, nothing.

109
00:11:00.425 --> 00:11:03.275
There is no universal threshold.

110
00:11:03.275 --> 00:11:10.975
A raw score depends on how long the sequences are, which substitution matrix was used, and what the gap penalties were.

111
00:11:10.975 --> 00:11:18.125
A score of forty is impressive for a short alignment under one matrix and unremarkable for a long one under another.

112
00:11:18.125 --> 00:11:21.450
You cannot carry a number between contexts.

113
00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:24.525
The intuitive fix is calibration.

114
00:11:24.525 --> 00:11:32.850
Take your two sequences, shuffle one of them — same length, same amino acid composition, no relationship — and align it the same way.

115
00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:39.750
Do that many times and you get a distribution of scores achievable by chance alone with sequences of this shape.

116
00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:42.700
Now ask where your real score sits.

117
00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:47.500
Far above almost all of them, and chance is an unlikely explanation.

118
00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:50.225
Buried in the middle, and you have nothing.

119
00:11:50.225 --> 00:11:55.475
For local alignments this can be made precise, and there is a subtlety that matters.

120
00:11:55.475 --> 00:12:00.700
The best scores of unrelated sequences do not follow a normal distribution.

121
00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:15.575
They follow an extreme-value distribution, with a long right tail — and that makes sense, because a local alignment score is the maximum over many possible sub-alignments, and maxima of random quantities have their own characteristic distribution.

122
00:12:15.575 --> 00:12:23.525
Treat them as normal and you will underestimate how often chance produces a high score, which means believing hits you should not.

123
00:12:23.525 --> 00:12:26.150
Database tools build on this.

124
00:12:26.150 --> 00:12:35.225
An E-value is the number of hits this good you would expect by chance in a database of this size — which turns a score into something you can actually interpret.

125
00:12:35.225 --> 00:12:38.325
Session five does E-values properly.

126
00:12:38.325 --> 00:12:41.225
One more useful rule, about length.

127
00:12:41.225 --> 00:12:50.950
Each aligned residue pair carries a certain amount of information in bits, set by the substitution matrix; this is the matrix's relative entropy.

128
00:12:50.950 --> 00:12:56.825
Multiply by the number of aligned residues and you get the information in the whole alignment.

129
00:12:56.825 --> 00:13:02.175
Roughly thirty bits is the threshold for an alignment to be convincing by itself.

130
00:13:02.175 --> 00:13:13.800
The practical consequence is sharp: a matrix built for distant relatives carries less information per residue, so finding a distant homologue needs a longer alignment, not merely a higher score.

131
00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:17.450
There is a limit to what a short sequence can ever prove.

